[{:seon.task/name "inspect-a-kept-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Runs are kept (target/check/<time>/runs, runs/, t5/out/*.store) and are evidence. A root opens a kept store for inspection: its reconciler observes and acts on nothing (no spawn, no kill, no judge) unless released, so a run's main and branches can be queried and its agents restored without resuming its work: `bin/root --inspect STORE`, with the MCP tools working against it. Nothing exists yet (bin/root has no --inspect; the proof is unwritten)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-inspected-run-starts-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "restore-and-replay-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Datahike keeps every commit as an immutable value with its parents (``datahike.versioning/branch-history``; `branch!` accepts a commit id; `commit-as-db` loads one): on a copy of t5/out/real3.store, `d/branch!` from the middle of task-t5-edit-forms's 19 commits (335 evals) gave a branch holding exactly its 29 evals. So branching from any commit costs no copy, and an eval between commits is reached by branching from the commit before and replaying. Restore: an agent forked on a kept branch at its end state (resume's load of the branch program). Replay to step N: an agent forked from the run's basis evaluates its recorded forms 1..N in order through its own REPL, every effect answered from its recorded outcome by a replay owner (nothing fires); each result is compared with its record and a divergence recorded (nondeterminism, or a gap in the record). The debugging agent works the re-creation through seon_eval, its new instructions a task whose :evidence names the source run, branch and point. Every form's exact text is recorded (`:seon.eval/code`, the request's whole text, on every eval); it needs effect outcomes recorded whole (the model's reply is on the turn; file and other effects' answers are not). Nothing built yet."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.server/inspect-a-kept-run"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-replayed-agent-reproduces-its-recorded-results
                      seon.server-test/a-divergence-on-replay-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "done-reads-its-reason-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (still true in src/seon/server.clj). What exists: the :done op's c/await-db pred tests the task's outcome (not in #{:running :ready :gate}) and c/defect, and after the wait `(and (= :running (outcome db)) (c/defect db t \"seon.reconcile\"))` tests the same two facts again to choose its answer. Correct: the wait returns the reason it ended on (the pred returns the verdict or the defect it matched), and :done answers that, so one read of the facts decides. Replaces the second read. Proof to write: :done answers the defect the wait matched even when main holds a later commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/done-answers-defect-verdict-wait-matched-even-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-tools-are-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: it deletes the ops agents, context, message, prove and done, which AGENTS.md section 6 and the MCP tools still document and use; overlaps seon.server/a-read-only-looks; the owner Sean decides whether those ops become evals before anyone builds, then it splits (dead-agent end in agents, one test through prove). The owner, 2026-10-30: the MCP tools are thin wrappers around Seon's REPL-based eval, so an outside session (a Claude) works as a Seon agent does and sees what it sees. Inside, a Seon agent has one tool, eval (text and code at its REPL), and every capability is a function it evaluates: (my.agent/prove), (my.agent/messages), (my.agent/problem sig), (my.agent/help ..), @ctx; each answer is one shape by the one printer (handle, value or error, :out, and a line of what changed: :main). Rule: every capability is a function an eval calls, none is only a tool. Outside: seon_eval {agent, text, code} (an agent's id or root) is the tool, through seon.repl/eval-string, origin :outside, its text the printed response a Seon agent's model reads; seon_attach {task} stays (a session needs a process, and attach records its client as holder); the ops agents, context, message, prove and done become functions evaluated on root or the agent ((my.root/agents), (my.agent/context), (my.agent/send-message ..), (my.agent/prove), (my.task/done!)) and their seon.server ops and op-answer methods are deleted, bin/ scripts and checks calling seon_eval. Each answer adds what its caller cannot see otherwise: for a Seon agent, the problems its eval caused (a test child that died in its prove, a refused write, a failed effect: root's records against that eval); for an outside client (bin/mcp stamps its client id), root's problem facts changed on main since that client's last answer (the main t it last saw, held by the server as a cache keyed by client, regenerable: read through Datahike's history, never my.root/problems' ~600 MB recomputation), as problems-model's grouped entries. A worktree's root is one more bin/mcp entry (its port argument), no new mechanism. Also from the 2026-10-30 proposal: a dead agent's end, exit and stderr tail answered by the agents function (root records them, seon.root's end-agent and record-death); one named test proved through the prove function (names take ns/test as bin/test does). Owned by the optimization session with the context session (the owner, 2026-10-30)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "outside-input-has-a-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: input from outside is parsed and dispatched with no Malli contract on its shape. seon.server/answer takes :map; respond sniffs the protocol from the line's first characters; each op's arguments are declared as JSON Schema in ops, a second schema language never used on an EDN request. seon.web's /call reads args by edn/read-string with no guard, checked first inside the web proc's step, so a malformed request is a step's throw, not a 400. seon.model's sse-reply, reply-of, wire-message(s) and request-body answer or take :map. my.root/example-txs re-states the session shape as a hand predicate (b5fabd00f0 added an arm). The change: :seon.server/request [:multi {:dispatch :op}] validated in respond, a request that is invalid answered, explained, with seon.contract/problems, and the MCP tool list's inputSchema derived from it (malli.json-schema), deleting the hand JSON Schema; the web edge reads args through the same validator and answers 400 with the problems; :seon.model/wire-reply (only the keys read) and :seon.model/wire-request; one :seon.ctx/session schema (the first transaction's agent requires :seon.agent/id and an example/ key) as seon.ctx.session/session-tx's output contract, with example-txs refusing by it, deleting the predicate. Docs: docs/model.md, docs/dashboard.md, the server's section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-malformed-request-is-answered-with-its-problems}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-read-only-looks"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: seon.server's :context op (op-answer :context) runs seon.turn/context-block with take? = (= \"outside\" (:model env)), the agent's model, whoever asks: a read from any client of an agent an outside client works takes its delivered messages (seon.turn/deliver! take?), writes its main and merge base, and records the read as an :outside model call, the agent's turn number moving. :context is not among seon.server/held-ops, so seon.server/held never asks whether the reader is the agent's holder. Reproduction (standing root, 2026-11-10): agent o21aa44adb8c304a (task seon.render/a-model-render-draws-each-turns-diff, model \"outside\", attached by one session) holds two :outside model calls on its branch, n 1 and 2, after a second session (mcp-tokens research) read its context; no message was ever sent to it, so none was lost this time.

What allowed it: a read that has an effect, chosen by the agent's settings rather than by the request. Siblings found by the sweep of seon.server's ops: (1) :context naming a task with no live agent attaches one (seon.server/attach! with the reader's :client), so a read creates an agent and makes the reader its holder; (2) every outside read writes a model call (the read entity) on the branch. :eval and :done act and are held (held-ops); :agents, :prove and :message read or act as named.

The change: a read never writes. :context only looks for every client (the request it would send now, made at the branch head without taking or recording), and an outside agent takes its messages when its turn opens, which seon.turn/outside-reply! already does (seon.turn/open-turn! delivers). A :context naming a task with no agent answers what attaching would show and attaches nothing (attach is its own op). Deletes: context-block's take? argument and its effects (deliver!, main-base-tx, the read's model call), and the read's recorded entity. The owner ruled (2026-11-10, Sean: \"A read never writes.\"): no record of a read is kept, on the agent's side or the reader's; the task that would have recorded reads as turns is removed.

Proofs, red first: a read by a client that does not hold the agent leaves its branch's datoms unchanged (no model call, no delivery, turn number the same); a message sent between two reads is taken by the next reply's turn and appears in the context after it; a :context naming an unattached task creates no agent. Web builds it after 2a; Context reviews what the model reads."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-read-by-a-client-not-holding-the-agent-leaves-its-branch-unchanged
                      seon.server-test/a-message-between-two-reads-reaches-the-next-turn
                      seon.server-test/a-context-read-of-an-unattached-task-makes-no-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reads-bytes-are-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (Optimization's named run of jolt/optbatch; its verdict cache holds 3 reds in about 80): seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did went red on 'in bytes too' (server_test.clj:445: later reads 4.37M to 4.47M bytes against the 1.05x bound of the read before), green alone. A cost bound that load or order turns red is a defect (docs/cost.md#what-is-measured: cost is work done). Read, not measured: seon.profile/measure! takes :ticks from the fiber (jolt.host/ticks-of, exact) but :bytes from the OS thread's allocation over the call's span (alloc-bytes before and after), and Jolt preempts fibers by ticks, so another fiber run on that thread during the call is counted. First step: measure one read with and without a second fiber allocating on the same carrier. Correct, if so: bytes counted per fiber as ticks are (Jolt's allocation counter per fiber, or measured where no other fiber can run), never a looser bound."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/a-calls-bytes-are-its-own-with-another-fiber-allocating}}
]
